    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "@leofang @emcastillo, my apologies again for the late reply. @leofang, your recommendation to use CUPY_GPU_MEMORY_LIMIT has successfully prevented swapping on Windows. Using cupy.cuda.MemoryPool.set_limit() works well too. I am currently working on rewriting this part of the code in CUDA:\r\n\r\n    while z < L:\r\n\r\n        gE2 = gp * cp.abs(field) ** 2\r\n        id_max = cp.argmax(gE2)\r\n        h = min(mnpc / gE2[id_max], L - z)\r\n\r\n        # Calculate Dop for reuse\r\n        exp_Dop_h= cp.exp(Dop_s * h / 2.0)\r\n\r\n        # First operators action (frequency and time domain)\r\n        field = cufft.ifft(cufft.fft(field, overwrite_x=True) * exp_Dop_h, overwrite_x=True)\r\n        field *= cp.exp(-1j * gp * cp.abs(field) ** 2 * h)\r\n\r\n        # Second operator action (frequency domain)\r\n        field = cufft.ifft(cufft.fft(field, overwrite_x=True) * exp_Dop_h, overwrite_x=True)\r\n\r\n        z += h\r\n        n += 1\r\n\r\n        #print(f'h = {h}, n = {n}, and z = {z}')\r\n        \r\n    end_loop = time.time()\r\n    \r\n    print(f\" [{datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%d at %H:%M:%S')}] Loop finished after {(end_loop - start_loop):.2f} seconds and {n} steps \\n\")\r\n\r\n    field = cp.asnumpy(field)\t\r\n\r\n    return field\r\n    \r\nWriting the fft and ifft kernels to use two GPUs in CUDA was not so hard, but the rest is proving to be more difficult. In my previous post, when I mentioned that I was unable to get low-level CuPy functions to efficiently distribute calculations across two GPUs, I meant the calculations were slow (I see CuPy's documentation warns about possible performance issues while using multi-GPUs). Perhaps soon I will gain access to A100 GPUs, which should allow me to revert exclusively to CuPy. I'll keep you updated on this as well. Thank you.\r\n    ",
